 ],
 "literal": "Like a waterfall, on the large rock splashing, / the river's fullness proclaiming, the man of the land having trusted, / what was experienced was indeed for one day; but that, / for very many days, shoulders embracing, / has become an affliction of the nature of snatching.",
 "translation": "Trusting the lord of the land where the river's flood splashes against large rocks like a waterfall, proclaiming its fullness, our union was indeed only for a single day. But now, embracing my shoulders for very many days, it has become an affliction that snatches away my life.",
 "modern": "அருவி போலப் பெரிய பாறைகளில் மோதிச் சிதறி, ஆற்றின் வெள்ளப் பெருக்கை அறிவிக்கும் நாட்டை உடையவனை நம்பி, அவனோடு நான் அடைந்த இன்பம் ஒரு நாள் மட்டுமே. ஆனால், அந்த இன்பமானது பல நாட்கள் என் தோள்களைத் தழுவி, இப்போது என் உயிரைக் கவரும் தன்மையுடைய நோயாக மாறிவிட்டது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her close friend (or to herself) in the Marutam (agricultural) landscape, expressing her grief over the hero's separation. She describes his land as a place where the river's abundant flood splashes against large rocks like a waterfall, announcing its arrival. This image of the roaring, splashing river symbolizes the hero's intense but fleeting passion. She laments that while their actual union lasted for only a single day, the longing and pain of separation have lingered for many days. This persistent grief has now become a consuming affliction that clings to her shoulders and slowly wastes her away.",
